==English==

===Etymology===
From {{af|en|profound|-ly|id2=adverbial}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/pɹəˈfaʊndli/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-profoundly.ogg|a=US}}
* {{hyph|en|pro|found|ly}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv}}

# {{lb|en|manner}} With depth, [[meaningfully]].
#: {{ux|en|He thought and wrote '''profoundly'''.}}
# {{lb|en|evaluative}} Very [[importantly]].
#: {{ux|en|'''More profoundly''', it has shaken our most fundamental assumptions.}}
# {{lb|en|degree}} {{1|deeply}}; [[very]]; [[strongly]] or [[forcefully]].
#: {{ux|en|From his childhood, she was '''profoundly''' troubled.}}
#* {{RQ:Haggard She|passage=Leo was sleeping '''profoundly''', and on the whole I thought it wise not to wake him.}}
#* {{quote-journal|1=en|year=2019|author=Li Huang; James Lambert|title=Another Arrow for the Quiver: A New Methodology for Multilingual Researchers|journal=Journal of Multilingual and Multicultural Development|doi=10.1080/01434632.2019.1596115|page=11|text=In fact, the influence of signage in a certain area may exist anywhere on a continuum from '''profoundly''' effective to utterly trivial or completely insignificant, irrespective of the intent motivating the signs.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Colin McCullough|title=Cheney warns of ‘profoundly negative impact’ if SCOTUS doesn’t resolve Trump immunity case quickly|magazine=w:CNN|url=https://www.cnn.com/2024/04/22/politics/liz-cheney-trump-supreme-court-presidential-immunity/index.html|date=2024-04-22|quotee=w:Liz Cheney|passage=Former Republican Rep. Liz Cheney said Monday if the Supreme Court does not resolve the issue of presidential immunity “quickly and decisively,” it will have a “'''profoundly''' negative impact” on the country.}}

